[#:seon.task{:name "contract-attributes"
             :intent "Give `seon.fs/attributes` a complete Malli contract that checks what its boundary is for, with the cheapest schema that checks it (`docs/seon/architecture/contracts-guide.md`): registered names, every input and output, the error variants it returns. A contract that refuses a real value (a reaching test, a live call, your probe) is a finding, not friction: if the contract misread the value's shape, fix the contract and cite the observed shape; if the producer emits a bad value, keep the contract, file a task on the producer naming `seon.fs/attributes` in its intent with the exact form, the refused value's essence and the Malli explain, and say so in your reply. Never widen to :any, [:maybe ...] or :map to go green. A caller that passes nil to an input is the producer of the bad value: change the caller so it does not pass nil (absence is no key), or take a map with the key optional; never contract nil with [:maybe ...] or :nil. Done when `seon.contracts/gaps` answers [] for it and its reaching tests stay green and no slower."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-path-under-a-root-answers-true"
             :intent "`seon.fs/under-path?` answers false where a path under the root should answer true: `(#'seon.fs/under-path? \"/\" \"/x\")` and `(#'seon.fs/under-path? \"/a/b/\" \"/a/b/c\")` both answer false, because it appends the separator to an ancestor that already ends in one. A descendant equal to the ancestor or lying under it answers true; a sibling sharing a prefix (`\"/a/bc\"` under `\"/a/b\"`) stays false. Read `seon.fs/under-path?` and `seon.fs/admit-destructive-path!`."
             :seon.task/children #{[:seon.task/sym seon.fs/prove-a-path-under-a-root-answers-true]
                                   [:seon.task/sym seon.fs/implement-a-path-under-a-root-answers-true]}}
 #:seon.task{:name "prove-a-path-under-a-root-answers-true"
             :step :prove
             :instructions "In `seon.fs-test` add a table test of `seon.fs/under-path?` `[ancestor descendant answer]` cases, including root \"/\" with \"/x\" (true), a trailing-slash root \"/a/b/\" with \"/a/b/c\" (true), \"/a/b\" with \"/a/b/c\" (true), \"/a/b\" with \"/a/b\" (true), and cases that must stay false: the sibling prefix \"/a/bc\" under \"/a/b\", and \"/a/b\" under \"/a\" (a descendant is not an ancestor). Tag it `^{:seon/tasks #{'seon.fs/implement-a-path-under-a-root-answers-true}}`. `seon.fs/under-path?` is private: call it through its Var, `(#'seon.fs/under-path? ancestor descendant)`. Run it red on your branch."}
 #:seon.task{:name "implement-a-path-under-a-root-answers-true"
             :needs #{[:seon.task/sym seon.fs/prove-a-path-under-a-root-answers-true]}
             :instructions "Make the table green by changing `seon.fs/under-path?` only: an ancestor that already ends in the separator is not given a second one, so \"/\" and a trailing-slash root contain what lies under them. Keep it safe: a sibling that shares a prefix (\"/a/bc\" under \"/a/b\") is never under."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-equal-root-and-target-have-no-intermediate-paths"
             :intent "`seon.fs/intermediate-paths` answers `[\"/r\"]` where root and target are the same path: `(#'seon.fs/intermediate-paths root target)` with both `/r` as `java.nio.file.Path`. The result is the paths from the root down to, and not including, the target, so a target equal to its root has none and answers `[]`; a target below the root keeps its root first (`/r` to `/r/a/b` answers `/r` and `/r/a`). Its one caller, `seon.fs/delete-recursively!`, reads the result only to refuse an intermediate symlink. Read `seon.fs/intermediate-paths`."
             :seon.task/children #{[:seon.task/sym seon.fs/prove-an-equal-root-and-target-have-no-intermediate-paths]
                                   [:seon.task/sym seon.fs/implement-an-equal-root-and-target-have-no-intermediate-paths]}}
 #:seon.task{:name "prove-an-equal-root-and-target-have-no-intermediate-paths"
             :step :prove
             :product #{seon.fs-test/intermediate-paths-lists-the-roots-from-root-to-target-exclusive
                        seon.fs-test/intermediate-paths-are-the-root-and-strict-ancestors-of-the-target}
             :instructions "In `seon.fs-test` add (1) a table test of `seon.fs/intermediate-paths` as `[root target expected]` cases, in that one order, each path a string turned into a `java.nio.file.Path` and the result compared as strings: [\"/r\" \"/r\" []], [\"/r\" \"/r/a\" [\"/r\"]], [\"/r\" \"/r/a/b\" [\"/r\" \"/r/a\"]], and a trailing-slash root [\"/r/\" \"/r/a/b\" [\"/r\" \"/r/a\"]]; and (2) a property law with `clojure.test.check/quick-check` and a fixed `:seed`: for a generated root and a generated list of segments appended to it, every element of the result is the root or under it and a strict ancestor of the target. Name the table `intermediate-paths-lists-the-roots-from-root-to-target-exclusive` and the law `intermediate-paths-are-the-root-and-strict-ancestors-of-the-target`, the step's declared product. Tag both `^{:seon/tasks #{'seon.fs/implement-an-equal-root-and-target-have-no-intermediate-paths}}`. `seon.fs/intermediate-paths` is private: call it through its Var, `(#'seon.fs/intermediate-paths root target)`. Run them red on your branch (the root = target row is the red one)."}
 #:seon.task{:name "implement-an-equal-root-and-target-have-no-intermediate-paths"
             :needs #{[:seon.task/sym seon.fs/prove-an-equal-root-and-target-have-no-intermediate-paths]}
             :instructions "Make the table and the property green by changing `seon.fs/intermediate-paths` only: a target equal to its root answers `[]`, and a target below the root keeps answering the root and each directory down to, not including, the target."}
]
